WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.000
Harnessing the power of Gods and fighting epic&nbsp;
battles in lava — sounds like a typical "Dragon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.040
Ball Super" experience! This is what you may&nbsp;
not know about "Dragon Ball Super: Broly."

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:16.980
Like the two preceding "Dragon Ball Z" films,&nbsp;
2013's "Battle of Gods" and 2015's "Resurrection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:22.620
'F,'" "Broly" is written by "Dragon Ball" creator&nbsp;
Akira Toriyama.The legendary creator had grand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:26.880
plans for the 2018 film: in an interview&nbsp;
with the official "Dragon Ball" site,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.440
director Tatsuya Nagamine revealed that, after&nbsp;
storyboarding Toriyama's original script,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.980
the movie was projected to run&nbsp;
for twice the intended length.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:40.080
"Broly" was planned to run for approximately&nbsp;
90 minutes, with Nagamine feeling that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.880
script Toriyama had written was enough to&nbsp;
cover two feature films. Nagamine worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.320
closely with the producer and the rest of the&nbsp;
production crew to trim down Toriyama's script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.060
to a more manageable 100 minute runtime while&nbsp;
retaining the "Dragon Ball" creator's vision.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:59.460
Nagamine has been a prominent animator and&nbsp;
filmmaker with Toei Animation for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:03.840
working not only on "Dragon Ball Super,"&nbsp;
but also on the long-running "One Piece"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.580
anime series and several of its films. With&nbsp;
"Broly" possessing its own animation style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:14.940
Nagamine recruited two of his prior collaborators&nbsp;
from the 2012 anime film "One Piece Film: Z" to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.020
work with him on the "Dragon Ball Super" project.&nbsp;
In the "Dragon Ball" official site interview,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.960
Nagamine cites both background animator Nobuhito&nbsp;
Sue and animation director Naohiro Shintani as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.440
prominent past "One Piece" colleagues he&nbsp;
brought on to work with him for "Broly."

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.440
While the animation team brought their&nbsp;
own visual flair to the production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:37.020
they were keenly aware that iconic imagery&nbsp;
such as Super Saiyan transformations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:43.080
Kamehameha Waves were firmly established in fan&nbsp;
expectation. Nagamine instructed the animators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:48.120
to feel free to use modern technology rather&nbsp;
than rely entirely on traditional methods to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:52.740
bring the movie to life. Nagamine reasoned&nbsp;
that, just as Goku has evolved and grown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:56.820
over the course of "Dragon Ball," so too&nbsp;
should the visuals behind the franchise.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.220
Though Toriyama wrote the screenplay for "Broly"&nbsp;
and provided character designs for the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:06.480
his contact with the production crew&nbsp;
was limited. According to the theatrical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.420
program for "Broly" distributed with&nbsp;
the Japanese release of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.400
Nagamine spoke with Toriyama only once during a&nbsp;
private advance screening for the film's staff.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.800
Nagamine admitted he did not consult with Toriyama&nbsp;
before starting production on "Broly," feeling he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:25.260
had Toriyama's tacit blessing to do the provided&nbsp;
script justice. Prior to "Broly," Nagamine adapted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:30.000
Toriyama's work with the "Dragon Ball Super"&nbsp;
anime series and the 2007 short film "Doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.680
Slump." Nagamine felt that these experiences&nbsp;
helped him understand Toriyama's storytelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:39.535
nuances and stylistic sensibilities,&nbsp;
and kept them in mind during production.

00:02:39.535 --> 00:02:41.460
Though Broly's past appearances in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:45.720
franchise had been considered non-canonical to&nbsp;
the overarching continuity, the Legendary Super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:50.580
Saiyan had become a significant fan-favorite&nbsp;
character. In an interview with the official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:55.380
"Dragon Ball" website, Nagamine revealed he&nbsp;
felt special responsibility as his mentor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.380 --> 00:03:00.420
Shigeyasu Yamauchi directed Broly's debut in&nbsp;
the non-canonical 1993 film "Dragon Ball Z:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:05.400
Broly – The Legendary Super Saiyan." This led&nbsp;
to the production crew taking a careful and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:10.380
methodical approach in animating Broly's action&nbsp;
for his canonical introduction to the franchise.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:16.620
Nagamine sought to evoke the feeling of raw&nbsp;
power and strength that Broly exuded in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:21.600
prior appearances. Nagamine worked on many&nbsp;
of the action sequences' storyboards himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:28.140
and estimated that 300-400 storyboards were&nbsp;
created for Broly's actions and movements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:34.680
alone. This thorough attention to detail made&nbsp;
for a carefully designed Legendary Super Saiyan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.536
with every action meticulously plotted&nbsp;
out as he battled the heroes in the film.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:44.700
While Toriyama provided character designs for&nbsp;
a long line of non-canonical "Dragon Ball Z"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:50.880
anime films, his input on their productions was&nbsp;
limited at best. While he helped design Broly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.440
for "Legendary Super Saiyan," he admitted in a&nbsp;
tweet years later that he wasn't really involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.820
in the production. When an editor suggested&nbsp;
incorporating Broly into the "Dragon Ball Super"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:05.580
era as the centerpiece of the 2018 movie, Toriyama&nbsp;
had to rewatch the character's prior appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:09.420
as he had forgotten important details&nbsp;
about the stories he was involved in.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:14.700
In revisiting Broly's past appearances,&nbsp;
Toriyama saw an opportunity to reintroduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:19.440
a different version of the character that was&nbsp;
more closely linked to the Saiyans' tragic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:24.360
history with Frieza. Toriyama kept the classic&nbsp;
image of Broly in mind from these non-canonical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:28.291
films while setting out to create a more&nbsp;
fascinating version of the character.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:34.560
Broly has historically towered over his&nbsp;
opponents, overpowering them with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.900
sheer might of his brute strength — and that&nbsp;
remains true of his redesign in the movie. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:44.100
the promotional program for the film's Japanese&nbsp;
theatrical release, Shintani recalls receiving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:48.480
the designs for the new Broly, feeling they&nbsp;
were noticeably different from his previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.100
appearances. With all of the character designs&nbsp;
that he received depicting Broly in armor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:57.600
Shintani added his own touch to the redesign&nbsp;
and wanted to get him out of his armor.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.920
Shintani revealed that the only design&nbsp;
directive he received from Toriyama was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:07.080
not to depict Broly as, quote, "overly macho"&nbsp;
in the final film. Taking that note in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:11.100
Shintani strove to depict Broly as&nbsp;
physically imposing as possible without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:15.137
going overboard in demonstrating&nbsp;
the Saiyan's overwhelming power.

00:05:15.137 --> 00:05:18.257
"Thank you very much. I am grateful."

00:05:18.257 --> 00:05:22.140
In the finished film, in contrast to&nbsp;
his bare-chested prior appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:26.100
Broly maintains his armor through&nbsp;
his fights against Vegeta and Goku&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.410
before losing it in a fit of&nbsp;
rage at the end of the movie.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:33.000
With the battle against Broly&nbsp;
lasting for most of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.900
the clash begins in an arctic location&nbsp;
on Earth before the snowy environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:42.420
is completely destroyed. The fighting only&nbsp;
intensifies, however, with exposed magma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:47.940
burning below the combatants before the skirmish&nbsp;
begins to shatter the walls to reality. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:52.200
this changes up the visual aesthetics of the&nbsp;
movie's environments and the fight itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:56.280
Nagamine had even more ambitious&nbsp;
plans for the film's climactic bout.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:01.380
In the theatrical program for "Broly's" wide&nbsp;
release in Japan, Nagamine revealed that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:05.940
had considered having the fight progress into&nbsp;
outer space near the Earth's sun or deeper within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.980
the planet's core. In addition to changing up the&nbsp;
scale of the battle, the settings would be a clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:16.260
homage to similar sequences in the non-canonical&nbsp;
anime film "Dragon Ball Z: Broly – Second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:21.871
Coming." However, these plans were dropped to&nbsp;
accommodate the final film's 100-minute runtime.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:26.640
Frieza plays a prominent role in "Broly,"&nbsp;
after being resurrected at the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:31.260
"Dragon Ball Super" anime series in exchange for&nbsp;
his assistance during the Tournament of Power. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:36.000
late voice actor Christopher Ayers provided the&nbsp;
English dub performance for Frieza in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:41.880
reprising the role he had held since the 2010&nbsp;
anime series "Dragon Ball Z Kai." Tragically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:45.180
Ayres' performance in "Broly" would&nbsp;
be the last time he portrayed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:48.540
character before passing away in October 2021.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:53.820
Ayres endured end-stage chronic obstructive&nbsp;
pulmonary disease and underwent a double lung&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:59.220
transplant to treat the serious condition prior&nbsp;
to playing Frieza in "Broly." The procedure and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:05.880
disease left Ayres with 11% lung capacity, meaning&nbsp;
the actor had to bring two to three oxygen tanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:10.800
with him to recording sessions. Ayres would take&nbsp;
himself off of his oxygen supply long enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.734
record a handful of lines as Frieza before&nbsp;
taking a break to resume his oxygen intake.

00:07:15.734 --> 00:07:21.074
"However, this is proving far&nbsp;
too interesting to pass up."

00:07:21.074 --> 00:07:25.260
This arduous process would similarly&nbsp;
be used by Ayres to record the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:30.120
final episodes of "Dragon Ball Super,"&nbsp;
underscoring his dedication to the role.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:35.040
Nagamine is a credited co-director on the&nbsp;
anime series "Dragon Ball Super" for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:39.360
latter half of its run, working with fellow&nbsp;
co-director Ryota Nakamura. While the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:43.980
stretch of episodes was produced concurrently&nbsp;
with "Broly," Nagamine intended to maintain his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:50.040
full directorial responsibilities, helming both&nbsp;
projects simultaneously to see "Dragon Ball Super”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:55.380
through to the end. Nagamine's intentions were&nbsp;
overruled by Toei Animation, however, who had him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:59.880
instead focus his primary efforts on directing&nbsp;
"Broly" rather than the television series.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:02.520
In an interview with the&nbsp;
official "Dragon Ball" website,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.960
Nagamine admitted he wanted to finish what&nbsp;
he had started with "Dragon Ball Super." but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:11.460
felt Nakamura did a good job closing out the&nbsp;
series. With the show also ending in 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:15.600
several months before "Broly's" release,&nbsp;
Nagamine was able to give the feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:20.781
film project his full attention while&nbsp;
Nakamura worked diligently on the series.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:26.160
In the "Dragon Ball" manga and anime, Bulma's&nbsp;
home and the headquarters of her family's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:31.140
company Capsule Corporation are based out of&nbsp;
West City. "Broly" features an early scene set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:36.120
at Bulma's seaside home as she entertains Beerus&nbsp;
and Whis while Goku and Vegeta spar nearby over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:41.700
the ocean. In designing this second home for&nbsp;
Bulma, Nagamine was personally inspired by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:46.380
recent family vacation he had taken on the coast&nbsp;
of Italy to add to the film's varied environments.

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:51.900
An early design for Bulma's coastal home&nbsp;
noticeably resembled the Amalfi Coast in Italy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:55.620
where Nagamine and his family had&nbsp;
visited. Eagle-eyed "Dragon Ball"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.620 --> 00:09:00.600
fans noticed the scenic inspiration from the&nbsp;
film when the background artists shared an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:04.320
early look at several environments for&nbsp;
"Broly" months ahead of its premiere.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:10.740
As Vegeta's duel against Broly escalates, the&nbsp;
Saiyan Prince transforms into a red-haired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:15.720
Super Saiyan God to even the odds against&nbsp;
the Legendary Super Saiyan. The movie marks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:20.640
the on-screen debut of Vegeta making the&nbsp;
transformation, despite him using the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:25.560
significantly more powerful Super Saiyan Blue form&nbsp;
throughout the anime series "Dragon Ball Super."

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:31.080
In episode 27 of "Dragon Ball Super,"&nbsp;
Vegeta shows off his ability to reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:35.820
the Super Saiyan Blue transformation,&nbsp;
surprising everyone but a proud Goku.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:40.140
"Yeah, and unlike me, Vegeta didn't&nbsp;
have the benefit of absorbing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:43.500
energy from the Saiyan ritual — he had&nbsp;
to figure it out all from scratch."

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:47.940
Vegeta's use of the Super Saiyan God&nbsp;
transformation in "Broly" finally shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:52.446
him unveiling the form in action, before&nbsp;
Goku steps in to continue the fight.

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:57.780
Before Goku and Piccolo teach Vegeta how to&nbsp;
perform the Fusion Dance so he and Goku can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:02.100
join together to form Gogeta, they maneuver&nbsp;
Broly into attacking Frieza to buy themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:07.440
time. Though no specific time frame is given&nbsp;
for the skirmish in the film, Piccolo reminds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:13.080
the two Saiyans that each fusion lasts for 30&nbsp;
minutes before they separate and can perform the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:18.660
dance again. With Goku and Vegeta botching the&nbsp;
Fusion Dance twice before finally mastering it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:24.960
this means Frieza battles Broly alone for over&nbsp;
an hour before Gogeta arrives on the battlefield.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:29.640
Whereas the film only provides glimpses&nbsp;
of the fight between Frieza and Broly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:34.800
the battle is expanded upon in the manga&nbsp;
adaptation. In his golden form, Frieza is able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:40.560
to actually deliver some palpable hits on Broly&nbsp;
that are enough to briefly stagger his opponent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:45.720
though he still finds himself outmatched&nbsp;
by the Saiyan's raw fury. In contrast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:51.120
Frieza endures the beating of his life before&nbsp;
Gogeta steps in to finish the fight for good.

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:57.840
"Broly" saw the feature film debut of voice&nbsp;
actor Aya Hisakawa in the iconic role of Bulma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:03.180
for its Japanese-language track. Hiromi Tsuru,&nbsp;
who had been voicing Bulma since the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:08.760
"Dragon Ball" series in 1986, passed away&nbsp;
in November 2017 from an aortic dissection.

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:11.640
"Broly" marked Hisakawa's full debut in the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:16.440
as the character only silently appeared in the&nbsp;
"Dragon Ball Super" series finale over the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:21.420
credits sequence. Following "Broly," Hisakawa&nbsp;
has continued to play Bulma in the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:25.920
including the film's 2022 sequel&nbsp;
"Dragon Ball Super: Super Hero."

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:29.820
As Goku — and, later, Gogeta&nbsp;
— fight against Broly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:34.270
the announcer is heard eagerly hyping up&nbsp;
the proceedings over the film's soundtrack.

00:11:34.387 --> 00:11:36.275
"KAKAROT!"

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:39.540
The decision to include this&nbsp;
auditory flair was inspired by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:44.040
Nagamine observing the lengths Mexican fans&nbsp;
went in their unauthorized promotion of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:48.300
"Dragon Ball Super" series finale. Goku's&nbsp;
climactic bout against Jiren in the anime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:52.920
series was publicly broadcast while mock-up&nbsp;
posters hyping up the fight were produced.

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:57.360
In an interview with Mynavi, Nagamine&nbsp;
revealed that these unsanctioned fan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:02.040
promotions inspired him to add a fight&nbsp;
announcer to "Broly" to evoke a similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:06.060
sense of energy. Nagamine felt that the&nbsp;
addition enhanced the enjoyment of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:11.820
fights and encouraged audiences to revisit them&nbsp;
to reignite that thrill in repeat viewings.

